    "problem_statement": "Query params property returns reference instead of copy\n\n#### Description\n\nWhen accessing query parameters through the `args` property, modifications to the returned dictionary directly affect the original furl object's query parameters. This creates unexpected behavior where external code can inadvertently modify the furl object's state.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom furl import furl\n\n# Create a furl object with some query parameters\nf = furl('http://example.com?foo=bar&baz=qux')\n\n# Get the args (query parameters)\nparams = f.args\n\n# Modify the returned dictionary\nparams['new_param'] = 'new_value'\ndel params['foo']\n\n# The original furl object is unexpectedly modified\nprint(f.url)  # Expected: http://example.com?foo=bar&baz=qux\n              # Actual: http://example.com?baz=qux&new_param=new_value\n```\n\nThe issue occurs because the `args` property returns a direct reference to the internal query parameters dictionary rather than a copy, allowing external code to modify the furl object's internal state without going through the proper interface."
  }
